  <div class="footnote" onclick="toggleFn('fn1')">
    <div class="fn-title">⚠️ How to read this reference <span class="fn-chev" id="fn1-chev">▶</span></div>
    <div class="fn-body" id="fn1-body">
      <b>Section 9(3)</b> — specified goods/services notified for RCM regardless of the supplier's registration status.
      <b>Section 9(4)</b> — RCM triggered specifically because the supplier is unregistered, for the notified categories only (the old blanket URD rule was suspended in 2018 and never restored).
      <b>Section 9(5)</b> — the e-commerce operator is deemed the supplier and pays GST on behalf of the actual service provider (not strictly "reverse charge" but grouped here for completeness).
      RCM tax must always be paid in cash through the electronic cash ledger — never through ITC — though ITC can typically be claimed back in the same return, subject to Section 17(5) restrictions. Rates, entries and notifications change periodically via CBIC notifications and GST Council recommendations; treat this as a starting point for research, not a final position.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="footnote" style="margin-top:0.6rem" onclick="toggleFn('fn2')">
    <div class="fn-title">🌐 Import of services — quick rules <span class="fn-chev" id="fn2-chev">▶</span></div>
    <div class="fn-body" id="fn2-body">
      <b>Legal basis:</b> Section 5(3) of the IGST Act read with Notification 10/2017-Integrated Tax (Rate); place of supply governed by Section 13 of the IGST Act (location of recipient, for most B2B services).<br><br>
      <b>Rate:</b> There is no single flat "import of services" rate — IGST applies at the rate the underlying service would attract if supplied domestically (SAC-driven). Most professional/consulting/IT/SaaS services fall at 18%; a handful of specified services carry lower rates.<br><br>
      <b>No self-invoice required:</b> Unlike RCM from an unregistered domestic supplier, the foreign supplier's own commercial invoice is treated as sufficient documentation — a self-invoice under Rule 36(1)(b) is not mandated for import of services.<br><br>
      <b>Related-party / no-consideration imports:</b> Taxable even without consideration under Schedule I, para 4 (services between related persons or distinct persons/branches in the course of business) — valued at open market value under Rule 28. Common triggers: parent-company cost allocations, management fee cross-charges, seconded-employee salary recharge, brand/royalty fees.<br><br>
      <b>OIDAR:</b> For B2B OIDAR (cloud software, database access, digital ads, e-learning), the Indian recipient pays IGST under RCM as above. For B2C OIDAR (foreign platform to an Indian individual/non-business consumer), RCM does not apply — the foreign supplier itself must obtain simplified GST registration in India and pay tax directly.<br><br>
      <b>Time of supply:</b> Earlier of (a) date of entry in the recipient's books, or (b) date of payment — whichever is earlier. Determines which month's GSTR-3B carries the liability.<br><br>
      <b>ITC:</b> IGST paid under RCM on import of services is available as ITC in the same return, subject to the service being used for business purposes and not falling under a Section 17(5) block.
    </div>
  </div>

